## Deploying an Ansible change

```bash
task infra:check                                   # dry run, the whole site play
task infra:check -- --limit <host>                 # dry run, one host
task infra:deploy                                  # the whole base layer (site.yml)
task infra:verify
```

`task infra:deploy` runs `playbooks/site.yml`, which is also the correct entry
point on a fresh cluster: it orders the certificate plays before the NAS plays,
and `nfs_tls` fails loudly if the wildcard certificate is not on the server yet.
The narrower tasks are for re-runs once that ordering has been satisfied once:

```bash
task infra:base            # base.yml only — users, SSH, packages, resolver config
task dns:deploy            # dns.yml — resolvers, then ACME issuance and sync
task storage:deploy        # storage.yml — the NAS alone
task proxmox:ha            # HA rules, resource pools, replication
task zfs:encrypt           # encryption mechanism + boot units, where pools are encrypted
```

For a single role, address the play directly:

```bash
cd ansible && ansible-playbook -i inventories/prod/hosts.yml \
  playbooks/site.yml --tags <tag> --limit <host>
```

Secrets are injected by the task wrapper (`op run`), so a bare
`ansible-playbook` outside `op run` fails on the `undef(hint=...)` guards in
`group_vars/all.yml`. Prefer the task; if you must run the playbook directly,
run it under `op run --`.

All roles are idempotent; re-running is the normal way to apply a change and the
first thing to try when a host has drifted.

### A green deploy that converged nothing

The multi-host plays in `base.yml` and `site.yml` carry
`ignore_unreachable: true`, so one down box cannot red the whole fleet run.
ansible-core does not record such a host as dark: with `ignore_unreachable` set
it increments `ok` and `ignored`, never adds the host to `_unreachable_hosts`,
and the recap prints `unreachable=0` with `ansible-playbook` exiting 0. A deploy
that configured **nothing** on a host would otherwise report success.

A probe/gate pair around those plays is what turns that into a red run:

- `_reachability-probe.yml` runs first and records the misses in the in-memory
  `deploy_skipped` group. Every multi-host deploy play targets
  `<group>:!deploy_skipped`, so a host known absent is not dialled again in each
  later play and cannot hard-fail on the first task templating a fact it never
  gathered.
- `_reachability-gate.yml` runs last and carries the probe's host pattern rather
  than `hosts: localhost`. `--limit` applies to every play's host list, implicit
  localhost included, so a localhost gate matches zero hosts under exactly the
  scoped CI deploy jobs it exists to protect. `tags: always` cannot rescue a play
  with no hosts.
- The ledger is a t0 snapshot, so the gate re-probes whatever is not already on
  it. `deploy_skipped` converged nothing; `deploy_lost` converged part of the run
  and is the more dangerous state to report green.
- A host knowingly out for hardware work goes in
  `deploy_expected_absent_hosts` (`group_vars/all.yml`), which downgrades it to a
  warning. The concession is then recorded in git and expires with a commit.

The plays outside the ledger — host metrics and log shipping, which also target
application and k3s guests — report their own misses in a `post_tasks` step
instead. Read that line before treating a green run as converged, and re-run
against the host once it is back:

```bash
task infra:deploy -- --limit <host>
```

