## Guest autostart and boot order

`proxmox_autostart_enabled`, `proxmox_startup_order` and `proxmox_startup_delay`
on a host entry decide whether a guest comes back after its hypervisor reboots
and in what order — the resolvers before the relay, both before the k3s nodes.
The `proxmox_lxc` and `proxmox_vm` roles apply them when they create a guest, so
for anything provisioned from this inventory there is nothing else to do.

For guests that already existed (created by hand, or carried over from before
they were in `hosts.yml`), reconcile them:

```bash
task proxmox:configure-autostart          # add -- --limit <host> to narrow it
```

It reads each guest's live config through its `proxmox_host` and writes only on
a difference, so it is safe to re-run and safe against a powered-off guest.
Leave HA-managed guests out of the groups it walks: HA starts them, so `onboot`
is meaningless for them and setting it invites two owners for one decision. The
`services` group is out of scope for the same reason. An appliance VM this
cluster does provision belongs in `app_servers` with `ansible_connection: local`,
a `vmid` and a `proxmox_host`, so the reconcile reaches it.

