## Per-host log-shipping alerts

**Alert index**

| Alert | Means | First move |
|---|---|---|
| `HostLogShippingStaleAll` | no journald line from ANY host in 45 minutes | the host-side collector unit on one host, then the Loki push IngressRoute, then the Loki pod |
| `HostLogShippingStale` | no journald line from the named host in 45 minutes | `systemctl status alloy` on that host, then the Loki push IngressRoute and the Loki pod |

`kubernetes/infrastructure/observability/loki/host-log-staleness.yaml` is
generated. LogQL cannot group by label and alert on absence, so per-host
coverage is one rule per host, derived from the `alloy_host` play by
`scripts/generate-host-log-staleness.py`. Run
`task flux:sync-host-log-staleness` after any inventory change and commit the
result: `task lint` (`lint:repo-sync`) and the CI `check-generated-files` job fail until you
do, including on a freshly generated repository.

What the generator emits per host:

{% raw %}```yaml
- alert: HostLogShippingStale
  expr: absent_over_time({job="journal", host="<name>"}[45m])
  for: 15m
  labels: { severity: warning, component: logging, host: <name>, instance: <name> }
  annotations:
    summary: "No journald logs from {{ $labels.host }} in 45m (alloy_host shipping stopped)"
```{% endraw %}

### Loki push emergency fallback

If the Loki push IngressRoute is broken but Loki itself is healthy, host
collectors can push straight at a node port. Two objects are needed, not one:
the NodePort skips the basic-auth ingress, but `allow-loki-ingress` still admits
port 3100 from the Traefik namespace only, so the push is dropped without a
temporary `ipBlock` allow as well. Flux reconciles neither object, so nothing
removes them for you.

```bash
kubectl apply -f kubernetes/infrastructure/observability/loki/nodeport.yaml

kubectl apply -f - <<'EOF'
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-loki-nodeport-breakglass
  namespace: observability
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app.kubernetes.io/name: loki
  policyTypes: [Ingress]
  ingress:
    - from:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{ lan_cidr }}
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 3100
EOF
```

The CIDR is a literal on purpose: `kubectl` applies this object directly and
Flux never substitutes `${cluster_lan_cidr}` for you.

Then point the host collectors at the node port and redeploy them:

```bash
# alloy_host_loki_url in group_vars/all.yml (or a host_vars override):
#   http://<node>:31100/loki/api/v1/push
task infra:deploy -- --tags alloy_host
```

Teardown, once the ingress is fixed: revert the `alloy_host_loki_url` override,
redeploy the collectors, then delete both objects.

```bash
kubectl -n observability delete networkpolicy allow-loki-nodeport-breakglass
kubectl -n observability delete service loki-external
```

