## Rotating a secret

**In-cluster** (anything an `ExternalSecret` produces):

```bash
# update the item in the vault, then:
task flux:rotate-secret -- <namespace>/<name>    # force sync + restart consumers
task flux:refresh-secret -- <namespace>/<name>   # sync only, no restart
```

Both take a `<namespace>/<name>` pair naming the **ExternalSecret**, not the app;
anything else is rejected. `kubectl get externalsecrets -A` lists the valid
arguments.

Restarting matters more than it looks: the operator re-fetches on a long
interval and the reloader deliberately ignores Secret changes, so a rotation
without a restart is a silent no-op.

### What each ExternalSecret holds

One row per shipped `ExternalSecret`, with the vault items it reads.
`scripts/check-secret-rotation-coverage.py` fails when a new one, or a new
`remoteRef.key`, is missing from this table.

| ExternalSecret | Vault items | After rotating |
|---|---|---|
| `observability/alertmanager-config` | `Alertmanager Webhook`, `SMTP Relay Auth`, `Healthchecks Watchdog` | `task flux:rotate-secret -- observability/alertmanager-config`, then send a test alert |
| `observability/observability-secrets` | `Grafana SSO` | `task flux:rotate-secret -- observability/observability-secrets`; the admin password only takes effect on a fresh Grafana database |
| `observability/loki-push-auth` | `Loki Push Auth` | `task flux:rotate-secret -- observability/loki-push-auth`, then redeploy the host log shippers — they hold the same credential |
| `authentik/authentik-secrets` | `Authentik Secrets`, `SMTP Relay Auth` | `task flux:rotate-secret -- authentik/authentik-secrets`. The PostgreSQL passwords must change in the database first, or the pods crashloop |
| `registry-cache/registry-cache-secrets` | `Registry Cache Upstream` | `task flux:rotate-secret -- registry-cache/registry-cache-secrets` |
{% if dns_backend == 'cloudflare' %}| `dns-api-token` (ClusterExternalSecret, fanned out) | `Cloudflare DNS Token` | rotate the token in the DNS provider first, then run `task flux:rotate-secret -- <ns>/dns-api-token` once per labelled namespace. `kubectl get externalsecrets -A` lists the copies. cert-manager DNS-01, external-dns and the DDNS CronJob all read it |
{% endif %}{% if vpn_tailscale %}| `tailscale/tailscale-operator-oauth` | `Tailscale Operator OAuth` | `task flux:rotate-secret -- tailscale/tailscale-operator-oauth`; existing proxies keep running on their issued node keys |
{% endif %}{% if git_backend == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}| `gitlab-runner/gitlab-runner-token` | `GitLab Runner` | register a new runner token in the forge first, then `task flux:rotate-secret -- gitlab-runner/gitlab-runner-token` |
| `gitlab-runner-privileged/gitlab-runner-privileged-token` | `GitLab Runner Privileged` | same procedure, on the privileged runner |
{% endif %}
`SMTP Relay Auth` is read by two of them, so rotating it is two rotations.

**Host-side** (anything injected with `op run`): update the item, then re-run the
task that consumes it. `task secrets:show` prints every reference this
repository uses, so you can see which item a given task needs before touching it.

**The bootstrap credentials** cannot rotate themselves. Regenerate the Connect
server credentials and re-run `task flux:bootstrap-secrets`, which recreates
both hand-made Kubernetes Secrets and mints a fresh Connect token.

