## Adding or changing a DNS record

The split horizon means a name has two halves, and which half answers depends
on who is asking. Change the one the client resolves, and keep the two from
disagreeing.

**Internal (`*.{{ internal_domain }}`)** is a rewrite entry answering the
internal ingress VIP. Add it to `_dns_service_rewrites` in
`ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/dns.yml`, apply with `task dns:deploy`,
then verify against each resolver:

```bash
dig @<resolver> <name>.{{ internal_domain }} +short
```

A rewrite added in the AdGuard UI is pruned on the next deploy: the role owns
the whole list. Codify it in `dns.yml` for anything permanent.

**External (`*.{{ external_domain }}`)** is normally an
`external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/*` annotation on the Ingress or IngressRoute,
so adding an app's public name needs no Terraform edit at all. {% if dns_backend == 'cloudflare' %}Only what
external-dns cannot express goes in `terraform/cloudflare/dns.tf` as an entry in
`local.dns_records`: the apex, CAA, SPF and DMARC, DDNS-tracked A records,
nested subdomains and DNS-only records. Those ship behind a plan — `task
terraform:plan`, then the apply job.{% else %}Only what external-dns cannot
express is declared by hand in the DNS provider's own configuration: the apex,
CAA, SPF and DMARC, nested subdomains and DNS-only records.{% endif %}

Keep both halves pointing at the same service. A name that resolves to the
internal VIP inside and to a stale public address outside is the failure mode
that looks like an app fault from one network and works fine from the other.

