## Certificates

**Alert index**

| Alert | Means | First move |
|---|---|---|
| `CertExpiringWarning` / `CertExpiringCritical` | an in-cluster certificate is near expiry | `kubectl describe certificaterequest -n <ns> <name>` |
| `BlackboxCertExpiringSoon` | the certificate a probe SEES is near expiry, whatever cert-manager thinks | check what the endpoint is actually serving before the issuer |
| `CertificateNotReady` | cert-manager has never issued this certificate | `kubectl describe certificate -n <ns> <name>` and check the issuer and DNS token |
| `CertRenewalFailed` / `CertRenewalFailedProlonged` | the host-side renewal exited non-zero, for an hour or for three days | read the reload script's journal on the certificate authority; the prolonged arm is the same work at higher urgency |
| `CertRenewalStale` | nothing has renewed for 75 days, so renewal stopped RUNNING rather than failing | check the renewal cron entry on that host: a dead cron never fails, so `CertRenewalFailed` stays silent |
| `CertDistributionTargetFailed` | the host-side renewal worked but one named target host did not get the new certificate | check SSH reachability to that host, then the reload script's journal on the certificate authority |
| `CertExpiringSoon` / `CertExpiringSoonCritical` | the host-distributed wildcard is within 14 / 3 days of its real notAfter, or its gauge is gone | renew and redistribute now; TLS-required NFS exports and log ingest break at expiry, not just web UIs |

```bash
kubectl get certificates -A                          # in-cluster
kubectl describe certificaterequest -n <ns> <name>    # why it is stuck
cd ansible && ansible-playbook -i inventories/prod/hosts.yml \
  playbooks/site.yml --tags acme_certs                # host-side renewal
task certs:show-host-keys                            # the pinned distribution host keys
```

Almost every failure is one of: the DNS token cannot see the zone, the challenge
record has not propagated yet, or the account hit a rate limit after a loop of
failed attempts. Check propagation with
`dig +short TXT _acme-challenge.<name> @1.1.1.1` before assuming anything is
broken.

Renewed host-side certificates are distributed over SSH with a pinned host key;
a distribution failure after a rebuild usually means the pinned key is stale —
`task certs:show-host-keys` prints the current ones to paste back into
`_acme_certs_targets` in `group_vars/dns.yml`. A target whose pin is **empty**
is filtered out of the push entirely rather than failing it, so it silently
holds an old certificate until you notice; `task infra:verify` is what names it.

The NAS depends on this: `nfs_tls` refuses to publish a require-TLS export
without the wildcard certificate and key in place, so a certificate failure
surfaces as a storage failure on the next `task storage:deploy`.

In-cluster, `infrastructure/configs/wildcard-certificates.yaml` holds the anchor
copy of each wildcard in `default`. Traefik reads Secrets only from the
IngressRoute's own namespace, so a namespace whose route needs a wildcard keeps
its own `Certificate` with a `renewBefore` staggered off the anchor. Per-host
certificates are a different identifier set and share no renewal window with
the wildcards, so a renewal storm on one does not touch the other.

**The FIRST certificate is issued by hand, once.** Ansible installs acme.sh, the
renewal cron and the push channel, and it never runs `--issue` — so on a cluster
that has never had a wildcard there is nothing to distribute, and the storage
host's `nfs_tls` stops on the missing file with an error that names a task which
cannot fix it. The order that works on a fresh cluster is: deploy everything
except the storage host, `task certs:show-host-keys` and paste the pins, then on
`dns-01` as root, with the DNS-provider credentials exported:

```bash
/root/.acme.sh/acme.sh --issue --dns dns_cf --server letsencrypt \
  --keylength ec-256 -d '{{ internal_domain }}' -d '*.{{ internal_domain }}'
```

then a full `task infra:deploy`. The certificate play prints that command
verbatim whenever no certificate exists yet, and every renewal after it is
automatic. This is a one-time step per cluster, not a runbook you return to.

