## When Flux is unhappy

| Symptom | First command | Usual cause |
|---|---|---|
| Kustomization stuck `Reconciling` | `flux get kustomizations` then `kubectl describe kustomization <n> -n flux-system` | a dependency stage is not Ready, or `wait: true` on something that never becomes healthy |
| `HelmRelease Ready=False` | `flux get hr -A` then `kubectl describe hr <n> -n <ns>` | values schema change across a chart major, or a CRD it expects is missing |
| `ExternalSecret SecretSyncError` | `kubectl describe externalsecret <n> -n <ns>` | item or field name does not exist in the vault — the event names what it looked for |
| Literal `${cluster_...}` in a live object | `task flux:lint` | key missing from `cluster-config`, or the Kustomization does not substitute from it |
| Pod `Pending` forever | `kubectl describe pod` | affinity selecting a node label that no node carries |
| Everything Ready, nothing works | `kubectl get svc -A \| grep LoadBalancer` | address pool exhausted or the VIP is not being advertised |

```bash
kubectl logs -n flux-system deploy/kustomize-controller --tail=100
kubectl logs -n flux-system deploy/helm-controller --tail=100
```

### A MetalLB VIP receives nothing while the service looks healthy

A frame addressed to a MetalLB VIP, rather than to the node's own address, is
FORWARDED to the guest and filtered by the per-guest firewall, after the host
bridge. It never reaches the hypervisor's own host-input chain, so a
datacenter-level rule compiles into the wrong chain and does nothing.

The receiver looks healthy throughout. Its listener is fine and injecting a
packet inside the pod works, so the only symptom is that nothing arrives.

Fix it with a per-guest security group in `group_vars/all.yml` — an `IN ACCEPT`
from the sender to the VIP on that port — assigned in `hosts.yml` to **every**
node the VIP can be announced from. A pod scheduled to a class of nodes but not
pinned to one lets MetalLB announce from any of them.

`MetalLBSpeakerNotScheduled` is the other shape of this. The speaker DaemonSet
wants zero pods, usually because no node carries the
`${cluster_node_label_domain}/ingress` label, so every VIP is still assigned and
nothing announces it.

